# Rust Serde Schema

`Lux.Rust.SerdeSchema` defines Rust-facing type contracts before values cross a
native boundary. A schema can generate Serde-ready Rust definitions, validate
Elixir values, encode JSON-safe payloads, and decode responses back into the
same Elixir shape.

```elixir
alias Lux.Rust.SerdeSchema

trade =
  SerdeSchema.struct(:trade_intent, [
    SerdeSchema.field(:user_id, SerdeSchema.primitive(:integer), rename: "userId"),
    SerdeSchema.field(:symbol, SerdeSchema.primitive(:string)),
    SerdeSchema.field(:metadata, SerdeSchema.primitive(:json), default: %{})
  ])

SerdeSchema.rust_definition(trade)
SerdeSchema.encode(trade, %{user_id: 7, symbol: "ETH-USD"})
```

Enums use Serde adjacent tagging by default:

```elixir
event =
  SerdeSchema.enum(:order_event, [
    SerdeSchema.variant(:accepted, [
      SerdeSchema.field(:order_id, SerdeSchema.primitive(:string), rename: "orderId")
    ]),
    SerdeSchema.variant(:rejected, SerdeSchema.primitive(:string)),
    SerdeSchema.variant(:heartbeat)
  ])

SerdeSchema.encode(event, {:accepted, %{order_id: "ord_1"}})
SerdeSchema.decode(event, %{"type" => "rejected", "data" => "bad checksum"})
```

The manifest output is suitable for Rust code generation, NIF planning, or
command-boundary metadata:

```elixir
SerdeSchema.manifest(trade)
```
